  - q: What is a rider clause?
    a: >-
      An additional clause attached to the printed form that adds to or overrides
      it: laytime details, sanctions, war risks, emissions costs, vetting and so
      on. Where a rider conflicts with the printed text, the charter party
      normally says the rider prevails, which is why riders deserve the closest
      reading.
  - q: Is BIMCO a charter party?
    a: >-
      No. BIMCO is an international shipping association that publishes standard
      contracts and clauses. Its war risk, sanctions and emissions clauses are
      often added to tanker forms, and its GASVOY form is used for liquefied gas
      voyages.
  - q: Can we use our own form?
    a: >-
      Yes. Many trading houses and refiners have their own form, often based on
      Shellvoy or BPVoy. Owners will review it before they make a firm offer, so
      send it with the order; a form owners already know is negotiated faster.
  - q: Which law and arbitration usually apply?
    a: >-
      Whatever the parties agree. English law with London arbitration is the
      most common choice in tanker charters; New York and Singapore are also used.
      The choice matters for how clauses are read, so do not leave it to the
      printed default without checking.
