Most tanker voyages are fixed on one of a handful of standard charter party forms: **Asbatankvoy** as the general voyage form, oil-company forms such as **Shellvoy** and **BPVoy**, and **Shelltime 4** for time charters, each extended by rider clauses, many of them from **BIMCO**. The printed form sets the framework; the riders and the recap carry most of the commercial detail, so the real work is in reading those.

This guide explains what each form is for and which clauses decide the money on a tanker or gas-carrier fixture. It describes the forms in general terms; the edition and the riders agreed for your fixture are what count.

## Why do tanker charters use standard forms?

Standard forms save time and argument. Owners, charterers, brokers, lawyers and insurers already know how the printed clauses work and how courts and arbitrators have read them, so the negotiation can focus on what is different about this cargo, this ship and these ports. A charterer who proposes an unfamiliar form should expect owners to take longer to respond and to qualify their offer.

## Which forms are used for tanker voyage charters?

{% routeTable caption="The common tanker and gas forms, in general terms" %}
{% table %}
-
- Type
- Where you meet it
---
- Asbatankvoy
- Voyage
- The general tanker voyage form, published by the Association of Ship Brokers & Agents (USA); short printed text, heavy use of riders
---
- Shellvoy (Shellvoy 6)
- Voyage
- Shell's form, widely used by other charterers and trading houses as their own base
---
- BPVoy
- Voyage
- BP's voyage forms, used by BP and adapted by other charterers
---
- Shelltime 4
- Time charter
- The reference time charter form for tankers, also used for gas carriers
---
- GASVOY
- Voyage
- BIMCO's form for liquefied gas cargoes
{% /table %}
{% /routeTable %}

Oil majors and large traders also use their own forms. They tend to be longer than Asbatankvoy and reflect the company's operations: vetting, inert gas, ship-to-ship transfers, security and compliance are dealt with in detail.

## How does Asbatankvoy work in practice?

Asbatankvoy is brief by design, and most fixtures attach a long rider. Three printed provisions come up in almost every negotiation:

- **Laytime and notice of readiness.** Under the printed wording, laytime starts six hours after the notice of readiness is received, or when the ship is all fast in berth if that is sooner. Riders often change what makes a notice valid.
- **Demurrage.** Demurrage is paid at the agreed daily rate, and the printed form halves it for delays from certain causes, such as storms or strikes. Charterers and owners frequently negotiate those exceptions.
- **Pumping warranty.** The owner warrants that the ship will discharge within 24 hours or keep a stated pressure at the manifold, provided the shore can take it. Discharge time beyond that, caused by the ship, does not count as laytime.

## How do Shellvoy and BPVoy differ from Asbatankvoy?

Oil-company forms write into the printed text much of what Asbatankvoy leaves to riders: detailed notice of readiness rules, ship-to-ship transfers, vetting and inspection, cargo retention and claims procedures. Owners often regard them as more favourable to the charterer in places; charterers value the predictability. When a trader uses its own adaptation of Shellvoy or BPVoy, compare it with the original, because the changes are where the risk moves.

## Which form is used for tanker time charters?

Shelltime 4 is the reference form for tanker period business, and many oil majors use their own time charter forms built on similar lines. Whatever the form, the clauses that decide the economics are the speed and consumption warranty, the off-hire clause, the trading limits, the bunker clauses on delivery and redelivery and, increasingly, the emissions and carbon-intensity provisions.

## Which clauses deserve a second read?

- **Safe port and safe berth**: who carries the risk if a nominated port or berth turns out to be unsafe.
- **Laytime, notice of readiness and demurrage time bar**: when time starts, which interruptions stop it, and the deadline and documents for a claim. See our [laytime and demurrage guide](/blog/laytime-and-demurrage-a-practical-guide-for-tanker-and-lpg-charters).
- **Quantity and tolerance**: whose option, and what happens if less cargo is available.
- **Heating and cargo retention**: the temperatures warranted for heavy grades and how unpumpable cargo is treated.
- **Sanctions and price cap**: BIMCO and company sanctions clauses, and the attestations required for Russian-origin oil. Our [compliance page](/compliance) explains our screening.
- **War risks**: BIMCO's war risk clauses (CONWARTIME for time charters, VOYWAR for voyage charters) or company equivalents, and who pays additional premiums.
- **Emissions**: who bears the cost of EU ETS allowances; see [EU ETS cargo allocation](/blog/eu-ets-phase-2-cargo-allocation-who-actually-pays).
- **Turkish Straits**: on Black Sea voyages, how waiting at the Bosphorus and Dardanelles is shared; see the [Turkish Straits guide](/turkish-straits).
- **Law and arbitration**: the seat and the rules, and any small-claims procedure.

## What should you send the broker about the charter party?

{% callout label="Before the first offer" linkHref="/contact" linkLabel="Send an inquiry" %}
Name your base form and send your standard riders with the order. Flag the clauses you will not move on (for example sanctions or vetting wording) and the ones you can trade. Owners then make offers on your terms instead of theirs, and fewer points are left to argue after the recap.
{% /callout %}

We draft the charter party from the recap on the agreed form, highlight every change from the printed text and from your previous fixtures, and keep the clause history for the post-fixture work. If you are still choosing the structure, start with [voyage charter vs time charter vs COA](/guides/voyage-charter-vs-time-charter-vs-coa); for the order of events, see [how to charter a tanker](/guides/how-to-charter-a-tanker).
